<div class="landing-frame community-frame"><div class="landing-hero">
 <div class="community-intro"><p class="landing-kicker hero-enter">ARTISANS &amp; NGOs</p><h1><span class="hero-enter">A living</span><em class="hero-enter">tradition.</em></h1><p class="landing-lead hero-enter">Meet the makers keeping Tamil Nadu’s crafts alive and explore how NGOs and craft groups can support their work.</p><div class="landing-actions"><a class="landing-button hero-enter" href="#our-why">Discover our purpose &#8595;</a><a class="landing-button outline hero-enter" href="#community-support">For artisans &amp; NGOs</a></div></div>
 <div class="landing-composition hero-enter"><div class="landing-orbit" aria-hidden="true"></div><img class="landing-main-photo" src="/images/kalamkari-workshop.png" alt="An artisan painting a Kalamkari textile"><img class="landing-side-photo metal" src="/images/wood-carver.png" alt="An artisan carving wood"><a class="landing-photo-caption" href="#art-forms"><span><small>IN THE WORKSHOP</small><b>Explore the craft traditions</b></span><span aria-hidden="true">&#8599;</span></a></div>
 </div></div>
<section class="tribute-block" id="our-why" data-motion><figure class="tribute-art"><img src="/images/thanjavur-painting-process.png" alt="Close detail of an artisan painting a richly ornamented Thanjavur panel"><figcaption>Thanjavur painting in progress &#183; Photograph supplied for TN ARTISAN</figcaption></figure><div class="tribute-copy"><div class="eyebrow">The art of Thanjavur</div><h2>Built slowly. Meant to glow.</h2><p>In this close view, a fine brush adds colour and definition to a traditional devotional panel. Thanjavur paintings are recognised for their vivid figures, ornamental detail and raised gesso work, often finished with gold-coloured foil that catches the light. Each layer asks for a different kind of attention: preparing the board, laying the composition, building relief and completing the fine painted details.</p><p>The photograph lets us honour the work at its human scale: the hand, the brush and the time that rarely appears in a finished-product photograph. The artist is not decoration around the object. The artist is the reason the object exists.</p><a class="text-link" href="/collection?craft=Painting">Explore Thanjavur paintings &#8599;</a></div></section>
<section class="artforms-section" id="art-forms"><div class="section-heading" data-motion><div><div class="eyebrow">Many crafts, many kinds of care</div><h2>Every tradition asks for its own patience.</h2></div><p>Explore the material, skill and hours that shape each piece.</p></div><div class="artform-grid">
<article class="artform-card artform-large" data-motion><div class="artform-photo"><img src="/images/kalamkari-workshop.png" alt="Kalamkari artisan painting a long patterned textile by hand"><span>Kumbakonam &#183; Kalamkari</span></div><div class="artform-copy"><div class="eyebrow">Drawn and dyed by hand</div><h3>Kalamkari textile art</h3><p>In this workshop image, the maker works across an extended cloth surface, balancing repeated motifs and flowing borders. Hand-painted Kalamkari depends on steady linework, planned colour and careful handling of the cloth. A large design may take many passes to complete; the maker must keep the story legible from one end of the fabric to the other.</p></div></article>
<article class="artform-card" data-motion><div class="artform-photo"><img src="/images/wood-carver.png" alt="Tamil Nadu artisan carving a detailed wooden figure with hand tools"><span>Wood and stone carving</span></div><div class="artform-copy"><div class="eyebrow">Shape follows the material</div><h3>Carving a form into being</h3><p>Carvers first read the block, plan its shape and remove material in measured stages. The rough silhouette comes before the fine edges, faces and ornament. A small slip can change a whole contour, so concentration and years of tool knowledge matter at every pass.</p></div></article>
<article class="artform-card" data-motion><div class="artform-photo"><img src="/images/clay-sculptor.png" alt="Sculptor refining the details of a handmade clay figure"><span>Clay sculpture</span></div><div class="artform-copy"><div class="eyebrow">Earth, water, touch and fire</div><h3>Clay shaped by feel</h3><p>Clay workers judge moisture by touch, build a balanced form and allow it to dry at the right pace before firing. The final finish can mean another round of smoothing, painting or decoration. The potter and sculptor in these photographs show both sides of the practice: useful vessels and expressive figures.</p></div></article>
<article class="artform-card" data-motion><div class="artform-photo"><img src="/images/potter-and-market.png" alt="Potter surrounded by a market display of handmade vessels, lamps and clayware"><span>Pottery and terracotta</span></div><div class="artform-copy"><div class="eyebrow">Everyday craft, enduring knowledge</div><h3>Vessels made for daily life</h3><p>From preparing and centring clay to trimming, drying and firing, a pot passes through many skilled steps before it reaches a home. The broad market display is a reminder that one workshop can make forms for cooking, storing, planting, ceremony and celebration.</p></div></article>
</div></section>
<section class="platform-why landing-heritage" data-motion><div><div class="eyebrow">Why we built this</div><h2>So good work can be found, understood and valued.</h2><p>Small studios and craft groups often have to make the work, photograph it, explain it, price it, answer messages and manage delivery with very little time. This platform brings the product story, maker profile and order journey into one place, so a customer can discover a tradition and a small maker can present their work with greater clarity.</p><p>We are proud to contribute a digital storefront and a thoughtful way to tell these stories. We do not take credit for the art: the skill belongs to the makers. Our role is to help more people see it, learn about the effort behind it and choose to support it.</p></div></section>
<section class="mission-grid" id="community-support" data-motion><article><div class="eyebrow">For artisans</div><h2>Your work, in your own words.</h2><p>Maker profiles can introduce a craft, district, materials and process. Listings can show clear photographs, price, dimensions, stock and care notes. Orders can be followed through their making and dispatch stages, helping customers understand the time and work each piece needs.</p><ul><li>A dedicated place to present your craft and products</li><li>Clear product and order information in one studio</li><li>A direct way for customers to discover your work</li></ul><a class="text-link" href="/for-sellers">See the maker tools &#8599;</a></article><article><div class="eyebrow">For NGOs and craft groups</div><h2>Make the support around a maker stronger.</h2><p>With artisans&#8217; consent, a community partner can help document product details, photograph work, prepare listings, support digital skills and coordinate packaging or fulfilment. A shared online catalogue gives a group a practical showcase while each maker keeps their identity and story at the centre.</p><ul><li>Support product documentation and digital access</li><li>Help small groups reach customers beyond local footfall</li><li>Make the maker visible alongside the object</li></ul><a class="text-link" href="/for-sellers">How the marketplace works &#8599;</a></article></section>
<section class="closing-tribute" data-motion><span class="closing-mark">&#2965;</span><div class="eyebrow">The hands behind the heritage</div><h2>May every piece travel with its story.</h2><p>Browse with curiosity. Ask about the process. Give the maker&#8217;s time the recognition it deserves.</p><a class="black-pill" href="/products">Discover Tamil Nadu craft <b>&#8594;</b></a></section>
<p class="source-note mt-4">Photo credits appear alongside the images where available. Materials and methods vary by workshop.</p>
